    claim: "Nahum shows that vengeance and fury belong to God’s character as surely as patience. God is not only slow to anger; he also pours anger out.",
    response: "Nahum is a single oracle against Nineveh, capital of Assyria, an empire famous for mass deportations, impaling and flaying its captives. Its message to the oppressed is comfort: “The LORD is good, a strong hold in the day of trouble” (1:7), and “Behold upon the mountains the feet of him that bringeth good tidings” (1:15). God’s “jealousy” (<em>qanna</em>) is the fierce love of a covenant partner who will not let his people be crushed forever, and his vengeance (<em>naqam</em>) is the ending of the oppressor’s reign. Nahum even quotes Exodus 34 (“slow to anger”), and the book of Jonah, about the same city, shows the other side: when Nineveh turned, God relented, and Jonah complained that God was exactly as merciful as that formula said (Jonah 4:2). Read together, the two books say that God opposes the violence of empires because he loves its victims, and that he would rather the empire repent than fall.",
    context: "Nahum is usually dated to the seventh century BC, shortly before Nineveh fell in 612 BC, and is addressed to Judah under Assyrian domination.",
