    response: "The Song of Moses is war poetry that celebrates God as Israel’s defender, and the very next verse explains what his vengeance is for: “the LORD shall judge his people, and repent himself for his servants, when he seeth that their power is gone” (32:36). Here “judge” means vindicate and rescue the helpless, and the song ends not with a sword but with healing: God “will be merciful unto his land, and to his people” (32:43; WEB “make atonement for his land”). The Hebrew <em>naqam</em> often means the restoring of order by a rightful ruler, the vindication of the wronged, rather than personal spite (this sense is argued by George Mendenhall and is debated). Most importantly, the New Testament quotes this verse only to forbid human revenge (Rom 12:19) and to warn the church (Heb 10:30), and Jesus, the full revelation of the Father, defeats his enemies by forgiving them from the cross (Luke 23:34).",
