    testament: "NT",
    text: "“Dearly beloved, avenge not yourselves, but rather give place unto wrath: for it is written, Vengeance is mine; I will repay, saith the Lord” (Rom 12:19, KJV).",
    claim: "Paul tells believers not to take revenge precisely because God will. Retribution is not abolished; it is reserved to God, who will repay the wicked.",
    response: "The Greek word is <em>ekdikēsis</em>, built from “justice” (<em>dikē</em>), and in the New Testament it regularly means vindication, getting justice for someone: the widow cries “avenge me” (give me justice) in Luke 18:3-8, and Paul uses the same word for the Corinthians’ eager desire to put things right (2 Cor 7:11). Paul’s point is that the wronged person can leave their case with God, who will set things right, and the very next sentences show what that looks like for us: “if thine enemy hunger, feed him… Be not overcome of evil, but overcome evil with good” (12:20-21). The “coals of fire” come from Proverbs 25:21-22, and their meaning is debated, but in context they are the burning shame that can lead an enemy to change, because Paul’s whole goal is overcoming evil, not paying it back. A God whose method is retribution would make an odd model for a command to bless persecutors (12:14); a God who overcomes evil with good makes perfect sense of it.",
