    response: "These lines are prayers, the honest speech of people under threat, and the Psalms give voice to the full range of human feeling toward God, including cries for enemies’ children to be dashed against rocks (Ps 137:9) that no one takes as divine policy. Hebrew poetry uses “hate” (<em>sane’</em>) for strong rejection and opposition, and it is true that God utterly opposes violence and the people who love it as long as they love it. But Jesus explicitly corrects the reading that God hates his enemies: “Ye have heard that it hath been said… hate thine enemy. But I say unto you, Love your enemies… that ye may be the children of your Father which is in heaven: for he maketh his sun to rise on the evil and on the good” (Matt 5:43-45). Paul agrees: “when we were enemies, we were reconciled to God by the death of his Son” (Rom 5:10). If God loves his enemies and asks us to be like him in this, then “hate” in the Psalms describes his total opposition to what destroys people, not a second, rival nature.",
