    response: "In Semitic idiom “love and hate” often means “prefer and not prefer”: Jacob “loved also Rachel more than Leah”, and the next verse says Leah was “hated” (Gen 29:30-31), and Jesus’ “hate his father and mother” (Luke 14:26) is rendered “loveth father or mother more than me” in Matthew 10:37. Malachi speaks of two nations, Israel and Edom, in history (“his mountains and his heritage”, Mal 1:3-4), exactly as Genesis 25:23 says, “Two nations are in thy womb”. Paul uses the text to show that God’s purpose of choosing a people for the sake of the world rests on mercy, not merit (Rom 9:11, 16), and his argument in chapters 9–11 ends here: “God hath concluded them all in unbelief, that he might have mercy upon all” (Rom 11:32). Election in Scripture is calling for the sake of others, as Abraham was blessed so that “all families of the earth” would be blessed (Gen 12:3), not a sorting of individuals into eternal love and eternal hatred.",
