    response: "Threefold repetition is a Hebrew way of saying “utterly” (compare Jer 7:4 and 22:29), and it does mark holiness as central. The Hebrew <em>qadosh</em> means set apart, utterly other, unlike anything else; the English word “holy” is related to “whole” and “hale” (healthy), which is a helpful picture though it is English, not Hebrew. What does this holiness do when it meets a sinner? Isaiah cries “Woe is me! for I am undone”, and a seraph takes a coal from the altar and touches his lips: “thine iniquity is taken away, and thy sin purged” (6:5-7; the verb is <em>kuppar</em>, atoned, purged). Holiness here does not punish; it cleanses and then sends (6:8). Hosea shows the same: “I will not execute the fierceness of mine anger… for I am God, and not man; the Holy One in the midst of thee” (Hos 11:9). God’s otherness is exactly that he does not retaliate as humans do.",
