    ref: "Proverbs 17:15; Romans 4:5",
    testament: "OT",
    text: "“He that justifieth the wicked, and he that condemneth the just, even they both are abomination to the LORD” (Prov 17:15, KJV).",
    claim: "God condemns judges who acquit the guilty. If God simply forgave without punishment he would be doing what he calls an abomination, so justice must be satisfied first, which is what the cross does.",
    response: "Proverbs is about corrupt human courts, where a bribe frees the guilty and condemns the innocent (17:23; Isa 5:23). Then Paul, who knew this verse, deliberately uses the same Greek verb (<em>dikaioō</em>) to describe God as the one “that justifieth the ungodly” (Rom 4:5). The difference is that a human judge can only declare a verdict; God’s justifying is creative: he sets the ungodly right, makes them right with himself and actually changes them (Rom 6:7 uses <em>dikaioō</em> to mean “freed from sin”). A corrupt judge calls wrong right and leaves things broken; God’s justifying leaves nothing broken, because he heals the wrongdoer and, at the end, vindicates every victim. Whether justification is mainly a declaration or a making-right is a long debate between Protestant, Catholic and Orthodox readers, but both sides agree God is not doing what Proverbs 17:15 condemns.",
