    claim: "This is the heart of penal substitution: Christ is the propitiation who turns aside God’s wrath, so that God can remain just (punishing sin) while justifying sinners. Love and justice meet at the cross.",
    response: "The word translated “propitiation” is <em>hilastērion</em>, which is the word the Greek Old Testament uses for the mercy seat, the lid of the ark where blood was sprinkled on the Day of Atonement and where God said “there I will meet with thee” (Ex 25:22); Hebrews 9:5 uses it in exactly that sense. Paul says God himself “set forth” Christ as the mercy seat, the place where God and humanity meet and sin is cleansed; God is the giver of the atonement, not the one who has to be appeased. God’s “righteousness” (<em>dikaiosynē</em>) in this passage, appearing four times, is widely read as his covenant faithfulness, his saving action to put the world right, as in Isaiah where “my righteousness” is parallel with “my salvation” (Isa 46:13). So “just and justifier” can mean God shows himself faithful precisely by making people right, not that his justice is one thing and his justifying another. Whether <em>hilastērion</em> means “propitiation” (turning away wrath, Leon Morris) or “expiation” (removing sin, C.H. Dodd) is a classic scholarly debate; the mercy-seat reading sits underneath both.",
