    q: "God’s holiness demands that sin be punished. Because he is infinitely holy he cannot simply overlook sin; his nature requires that it receive its due.",
    a: "Holiness does mean that God cannot overlook sin, if “overlook” means pretending it is harmless. But Scripture shows what holiness does with sin when it meets it: it purges, as with Isaiah’s coal (Isa 6:7), and it refuses to destroy: “I will not execute the fierceness of mine anger… for I am God, and not man; the Holy One in the midst of thee” (Hos 11:9). In Jesus, the Holy One of God touched lepers and the unclean, and instead of becoming unclean he made them clean (Mark 1:40-42; 5:25-34). Holiness requires that sin be dealt with, ended, removed, healed; it does not require that a penalty be inflicted. The demand of holiness is satisfied when sinners become holy, “partakers of his holiness” (Heb 12:10).",
