    q: "What about Hitler? Surely you cannot say that God simply loves and restores the perpetrator of the Holocaust. Some evil deserves retribution.",
    a: "The question is right to feel moral horror, and any honest answer begins with the victims, not the perpetrator. The relational gospel does not say that what Hitler did will be glossed over; it says that God’s love is a fire that will bring every deed into the light (1 Cor 3:13; Luke 12:2-3), and for a man like that, facing the full truth of what he did, in the presence of the Love he violated and the people he murdered, would be the most terrible judgment imaginable. Whether such a person can or will finally be healed is a question many in this stream hold in hope rather than certainty. But notice what the objection asks for: that God’s goodness be measured by the worst person we can name rather than by Christ, who prayed for his executioners (Luke 23:34). If God’s mercy has a limit, the question becomes who sets it, and history shows that humans set it very quickly and very wrongly.",
