    a: "This is a fair warning that every generation should hear, since every generation is tempted to make God in its image. But the central claims here are ancient, not modern: Irenaeus and Athanasius saw salvation as healing and restoring the image of God; Gregory of Nyssa and Isaac the Syrian taught that God’s purposes are remedial; Aquinas held that God’s justice is founded on his mercy. And it could just as easily be said that the penal model reflects its own cultures: the feudal honour of Anselm’s world and the legal-penal frameworks of early modern Europe. The real test is not whether an idea is modern or ancient, but whether it fits Jesus, who is “the image of the invisible God” (Col 1:15). The God of the relational gospel is not soft; he is the God of the cross, who absorbs evil and overcomes it with good.",
