    a: "This is a sophisticated reply, and it rightly rejects the “two natures in tension” picture: Aquinas teaches that God has no parts and his attributes are identical with his essence (ST I, q.3). But simplicity cuts both ways: if God’s justice is identical with his love, then his justice must be recognisably loving, aimed at the good of the one judged. Aquinas himself taught that in every work of God there is mercy and justice, and that the work of justice always presupposes and is founded on mercy (ST I, q.21, a.4). A “love” that eternally inflicts suffering for no good to the sufferer would stretch the word beyond meaning, which is why MacDonald argued that if mercy could ever be “too much” for God, mercy could not be a divine attribute at all. Simplicity is a strong friend of the relational view: there is one God, and he is love.",
