    a: "It is true that the sharpest warnings in the New Testament come from Jesus, and that should be taken with full seriousness. But look at his words carefully: “hell” mostly translates Gehenna, the valley outside Jerusalem associated with child sacrifice and with the prophets’ warnings of the city’s destruction (Jer 7:31-34), and many scholars see some of these warnings as pointing at least partly to the catastrophe of AD 70. Look also at whom he warned: mostly the religious who excluded others, the rich who ignored the poor, and those who caused little ones to stumble (Matt 23:33; Luke 16:19-31; Mark 9:42-48). His warnings are real and severe, but he never presents them as the Father’s desire; the Father seeks the lost “until he find it” (Luke 15:4). And the same Jesus said that when he was lifted up he would “draw all men unto me” (John 12:32).",
