    a: "It is fair to say the relational view points that way, and many of its voices, from Gregory of Nyssa and Isaac the Syrian to MacDonald and David Bentley Hart, openly hope or argue that God will restore all. But the claim that God is love and that his justice is restorative does not by itself settle whether every person will finally accept that love; some in this stream hold a hopeful universalism, some a firm one, and some think human freedom may refuse forever. What is ruled out is the idea that God’s own purpose in judgment is endless retribution for its own sake. Scripture speaks of God desiring all people to be saved (1 Tim 2:4), of all things reconciled (Col 1:20), and of God being “all in all” (1 Cor 15:28); it also gives real warnings, and both should be held honestly. The debate is best had openly, not by the label “universalism” as if that settled it.",
